{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is home staging a way of hiding problems?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Staging is presentation: it edits furniture, scale, and light so a buyer can read the space clearly. Concealment is different, and in California it is also a legal problem, because sellers must disclose known material defects on the Transfer Disclosure Statement regardless of how the house is furnished. A rug placed to define a seating area is staging. A rug placed over a crack the seller knows about is not."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the biggest red flag when touring a home?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Water. Moisture problems are the most common expensive surprise and the most commonly papered over. Musty smells, isolated patches of fresh paint, stained or bowed baseboards, and efflorescence on concrete all point at water that has been somewhere it should not be. Structural movement runs a close second, especially horizontal cracks rather than hairline vertical ones."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does fresh paint always mean something is being hidden?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Most sellers repaint the whole interior before listing, and that is normal and expected. What is worth a question is partial fresh paint: one wall, one ceiling patch, or the inside of a single closet, painted while everything around it is older. Ask when it was painted and why, and note the answer for your inspector."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What should Orange County buyers check that buyers elsewhere might not?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Most Orange County homes are slab-on-grade with no basement, so movement shows up as cracked floor tile, sticking doors, and out-of-square door frames rather than in a foundation wall you can walk up to. Also check the Natural Hazard Disclosure for wildfire and flood zones, confirm whether the property carries Mello-Roos special taxes, read the HOA documents, verify permits on any room addition or converted garage, and find out whether solar panels are owned or on a lease you would assume."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Should I still get an inspection if the house looks perfect?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, and a beautifully presented house is not a reason to skip one. A walkthrough tells you what to ask about; an inspection tells you what is actually there. Consider adding specialist inspections such as sewer lateral scoping, roof, and a structural engineer if anything on this list turned up."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Is a home that has been relisted or repeatedly reduced a bad sign?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not automatically, but it is worth understanding. A listing that goes into escrow and comes back more than once often failed someone else's inspection or appraisal. Ask your agent to pull the listing history and, if possible, find out why the previous deals fell through. Sometimes the answer is financing, which tells you nothing about the house."
      }
    }
  ]
}
